commit 1000662c31c37dcf1409b8514669cd490c417db9
Author: givis <givis@local>
Date:   Tue Nov 3 18:55:51 2026 +0000

    prefix-replay-depth: partial-hit CoW consumption must never kill the engine
    
    The producer-side record (block_idx, source_block) assumed the running
    request block table is append-only between the tail publication and the
    next allocation. Under replay traffic this broke once (18:22 UTC crash:
    allocate_new_blocks assert req_blocks[block_idx] is source_block ->
    EngineDeadError, container auto-restarted, prefix cache lost).
    
    Consume defensively: validate both identities (table slot AND producer
    marker) BEFORE any mutation; on mismatch retract the unproven published
    hash via _maybe_evict_cached_block (same tool the checkpoint branch
    uses), keep cow_block as a plain new block, and log a warning with the
    full block ids. Worst case one landing state goes unpublished and the
    next replay of that prefix hits shallower.
    
    Also remove the permanent cache-diag scaffolding (hit-diag, cache-diag,
    rekey-diag, mamba-lookup, hash-ins, hash-rm) from all three files: its
    env gate checked truthiness so VLLM_CACHE_DIAG=0 kept it always on; the
    noise obscured real errors and cost per-step scheduler work.

